theory_blocks.body:
If there are many different values, or the data is continuous, a list of every separate value is too long to be useful. Instead the data is sorted into **classes** (or groups), and we record the **class frequency** — how many values fall in each class.

Good class intervals satisfy three rules:

- every value belongs to exactly one class (no **gaps** and no **overlaps**);
- the classes usually have the same width;
- for continuous data, use inequalities such as $0\le m<2$ (“$0$ or more but less than $2$”).

For data that can only take whole-number values with a genuine gap between consecutive classes, a plain range such as $1$–10, $11$–20 is acceptable, since no value lies between $10$ and $11$.

